        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b696f513-e531-5b44-8c85-2e97263a02a4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Roger Casement\n- Places: Rahoneen, McKenna's Fort, Casement's Fort\n- Concepts: Celtic ringfort, Irish independence, human rights investigations\n- Events: Casement Report on the Congo, Easter Rising planning, Casement's capture and execution\n\nFacts:\n- Rahoneen is notable as the location of an ancient Celtic ringfort.\n- The ringfort in Rahoneen was once known as McKenna's Fort.\n- Roger Casement was honoured in 1905 for the Casement Report on the Congo.\n- Roger Casement was knighted in 1911 for his investigations of human rights abuses in Peru.\n- Roger Casement has been described as the father of twentieth-century human rights investigations.\n- In 1916, Roger Casement arranged delivery of German weapons as part of a plan to arm Irish rebels and create a rising against British rule to achieve independence.\n- When Roger Casement was too weak to travel, he was discovered while hiding at McKenna's Fort.\n- After Roger Casement was captured and executed for treason, sabotage, and espionage against the Crown, McKenna's Fort became known as Casement's Fort.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9c1f748f-2650-57ad-ba76-b462e223f5b6",
        "text": "Page: Rahoneen. Sentence: It is notable as the location of an ancient Celtic ringfort; at one time this was known as McKenna's fort, but, after the capture and execution of Roger Casement for treason, sabotage and espionage against the Crown, it became known as Casement's Fort.\nPage: Rahoneen. Sentence: \"Too weak to travel, he was discovered at McKenna's Fort where he had hidden after arranging delivery of German weapons as part of a plan of arming Irish rebels and creating a rising in 1916 against the British to achieve independence.\nPage: Roger Casement. Sentence: Described as the \"father of twentieth-century human rights investigations\", he was honoured in 1905 for the Casement Report on the Congo and knighted in 1911 for his important investigations of human rights abuses in Peru.",
